<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Italy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1555">1555</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Supreme Council of Italy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> was a council of the Spanish monarchy, created under Philip II in the 1550s to advise the king on the government of his Italian possessions: the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> and the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>. Sitting in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, it dealt with appointments, finance, justice and petitions from these states. It lost its purpose when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> gave up its Italian lands in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the council existed, the Spanish possessions in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> were administered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-aragon">Council of Aragon</a>, since the Crown of Aragon had long ruled most of them. Local councils and viceroys in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palermo">Palermo</a>, and a governor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, controlled their internal affairs. To coordinate Spanish rule in Italy better, Philip II separated the Italian states from the Council of Aragon, and Naples, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and Milan passed to the new council. The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> stayed under the Council of Aragon, as its own Stamenti demanded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Officially the Royal and Supreme Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, the council was second only to the monarch in governing these lands, which also included the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/state-of-the-presidi">State of the Presidi</a> and, until 1713, the Marquisate of Finale. It had a president drawn from the Spanish high nobility and six regents, two each for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, one Spanish and one a native of the territory, all experts in canon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a>. Three secretaries, a fiscal advocate who looked after the interests of the state and a conservator general of the crown's patrimony completed its staff.</p><p>Its role was solely consultative. It nominated the viceroys of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and the governors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> to the Spanish Council of State, over which the king presided, and approved the more important civil and military appointments. Its opinion went to the king in a document, the consulta, edited by the secretary, which he annotated with his own decision; when the council was not unanimous, every member's opinion was reported.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> the former Spanish states in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> passed to the Austrian Habsburgs, and in 1713 Emperor Charles VI set up a Supreme Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> to assert his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over them. Left without any territories to govern, the Council of Italy was abolished by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-v-of-spain">Philip V of Spain</a> on 1 May 1717.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Italy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After the last Sforza duke, Francesco II, died in 1535, Charles V kept the duchy and invested his son Philip; Spanish governors, the Senate and the <b>Council of Italy</b> in Madrid ruled it until 1706.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
